APX.cn = {"country":{"name":"China","tagline":"The world's third-largest vineyard and its oldest fermented drink, now remaking itself from the desert edge of the Helan Mountains.","classification":"China's wine GIs are protected under national systems (the former AQSIQ geographical-indication product scheme, now run by the China National Intellectual Property Administration), with production standards for named areas such as 'Helan Mountain East Foothill' (贺兰山东麓葡萄酒), 'Yantai' (烟台葡萄酒), 'Shacheng' (沙城葡萄酒), 'Changli' (昌黎葡萄酒) and 'Huanren Icewine' (桓仁冰酒). Four of them (Helan Mountain East Foothill, Yantai, Shacheng and Huanren Icewine) are protected in Europe under the EU-China agreement in force since March 2021. Boundaries are generally administrative and not published as precise maps, so in this atlas they appear as points.\n\nNingxia also runs China's only official estate classification. Introduced in 2013, when ten wineries became Fifth Growths, it was expanded in 2016 to five tiers (Fifth to First Growth) and is reviewed every two years by a panel of experts, with First Growths to be re-examined every ten years. By 2021 nine estates had reached Second Growth (including Helan Qingxue, Kanaan, Chateau Hedong, Legacy Peak, Lisi and Chateau Yuquan) and the First Growth tier was still empty. Beyond that, labels rely on producer reputation; much Chinese wine carries no regional claim at all."},"regions":[{"name":"Ningxia","slug":"ningxia","tagline":"A desert strip between the Yellow River and the Helan Mountains that became China's showcase for fine wine.","ids":["cn-helan-mountain-east-foothill"]},{"name":"Xinjiang","slug":"xinjiang","tagline":"China's largest wine-grape province, oasis vineyards on the old Silk Road between the Tianshan and the deserts.","ids":["cn-tianshan-north-foothills","cn-turpan","cn-yanqi-basin"]},{"name":"Shandong","slug":"shandong","tagline":"The cradle of modern Chinese wine, on a monsoon-lashed peninsula between two seas.","ids":["cn-yantai"]},{"name":"Hebei","slug":"hebei","tagline":"Two very different wine areas: the dry Huailai basin beyond the Great Wall, and the coastal Changli hills.","ids":["cn-changli","cn-shacheng"]},{"name":"Yunnan","slug":"yunnan","tagline":"Vineyards at 2,000 to 2,600 m in Tibetan valleys under the Meili Snow Mountains.","ids":["cn-shangri-la-deqin"]},{"name":"Gansu","slug":"gansu","tagline":"Vineyards along the Hexi Corridor, the Silk Road route between desert and the Qilian Mountains.","ids":["cn-hexi-corridor"]},{"name":"Liaoning","slug":"liaoning","tagline":"Icewine on Hanlong Lake, near the North Korean border.","ids":["cn-huanren-icewine"]}],"items":{"cn-changli":{"name":"Changli","cat":"GI","subcat":"National GI","region":"Hebei","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Marselan, Merlot, Chardonnay","summary":"A coastal producing area near Qinhuangdao on the Bohai Sea, protected as a national GI. It holds the Huaxia Great Wall winery, whose Cabernet was one of China's first serious varietal reds.","terroir":"Sandy soils near the coast; maritime climate with summer monsoon rain.","producers":["Huaxia Great Wall","Chateau Bolongbao"],"rslug":"hebei"},"cn-helan-mountain-east-foothill":{"name":"Helan Mountain East Foothill","cat":"GI","subcat":"National GI (EU-protected)","region":"Ningxia","created":"EU protection 2021","area_ha":40400,"styles":["red","white","sparkling","rose"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Marselan, Cabernet Gernischt, Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Riesling","summary":"China's best-known wine GI, covering the gravelly alluvial fans along the eastern foot of the Helan Mountains in Ningxia, from Shizuishan through Yinchuan's Xixia district, Yongning and Qingtongxia to Hongsipu. In 2026 it had about 606,000 mu (40,400 ha) of vines and 131 wineries, by its own count nearly 40 percent of China's estate vineyard. The GI was among four Chinese wine names protected in Europe when the EU-China agreement came into force in March 2021.\n\nThe region's rise has been rapid. The provincial government reclaimed desert for vineyards from the late 1990s, and small estates arrived in the 2000s: Silver Heights (Emma Gao, trained in Bordeaux), Helan Qingxue (whose Jiabeilan 2009, made with consultant Li Demei, won a Decanter international trophy in 2011), Kanaan, Domaine Pushang, Chateau Yuanshi and Chateau Hedong. Big groups followed: Changyu built Chateau Changyu Moser XV with Austrian Lenz Moser, Great Wall built Tianfu, and Moët Hennessy opened Chandon Ningxia for sparkling wine in 2013. The region's five-tier estate classification, begun in 2013, has nine Second Growths as of 2021 and no First Growth yet.\n\nThe wines are typically ripe but fresh Bordeaux blends and Cabernet Sauvignon, with polished tannins, plus Marselan, Pinot Noir and Chardonnay. The challenge is the climate: winters reach minus 20 degrees C or colder, so vines are buried under soil from November to April, which limits vine age and adds cost.","terroir":"Altitude about 1,100 to 1,200 m; roughly 200 mm of annual rain and 3,000 hours of sun; large day-night temperature swings. Gravelly, sandy alluvial fans with some loess, low in organic matter; irrigation from the Yellow River.","producers":["Silver Heights","Helan Qingxue","Kanaan","Domaine Pushang","Chateau Changyu Moser XV","Chandon Ningxia","Chateau Yuanshi (Zhihui Yuanshi)","Chateau Hedong","Legacy Peak","Lisi","Xige Estate","Longyu Estate","Ho-Lan Soul","Great Wall Tianfu"],"nerd_note":"Burying vines each winter means many Ningxia vineyards are trained with a slanted trunk ('crawling' or 'Ningxia-style' training) so the vine can be bent over and covered with soil.","rslug":"ningxia"},"cn-hexi-corridor":{"name":"Hexi Corridor","cat":"Producing area","subcat":"Producing area (no EU-protected GI)","region":"Gansu","styles":["red","white","sweet"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Riesling","summary":"A narrow Silk Road corridor through Gansu between the Qilian Mountains and the Gobi, through Wuwei (ancient Liangzhou, whose wine Tang poets praised), Zhangye and Jiayuguan. Modern vineyards, buried each winter, supply wineries such as Mogao and Ziyuan.","terroir":"Arid, continental and high (1,400 to 1,800 m); snowmelt irrigation; loess and gravel.","producers":["Mogao","Ziyuan","Qilian Winery"],"rslug":"gansu"},"cn-huanren-icewine":{"name":"Huanren Icewine","cat":"GI","subcat":"National GI (EU-protected)","region":"Liaoning","created":"EU protection 2021","styles":["sweet"],"grapes":"Vidal, Beibinghong","summary":"A GI for icewine from Huanren County in Liaoning, near the North Korean border, where grapes (mostly Vidal) are left on the vine to freeze and pressed while frozen, concentrating sugar and acid. Changyu's Golden Icewine Valley on Hanlong Lake is the best-known producer. It is one of four Chinese wine GIs protected in the EU.","terroir":"Cold continental winters with a moderating lake; vineyards at about 380 m.","producers":["Changyu Golden Icewine Valley","Huanren Zhangyu"],"rslug":"liaoning"},"cn-shacheng":{"name":"Shacheng","cat":"GI","subcat":"National GI (EU-protected)","region":"Hebei","created":"EU protection 2021","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Longyan (Dragon's Eye), Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Chardonnay","summary":"The GI for the Huailai basin around Shacheng town in Hebei, north-west of Beijing behind the Great Wall, along the Sanggan River and Guanting Reservoir. The state-owned Great Wall winery (founded 1983, now COFCO) is based here, and the area's centuries-old Longyan (Dragon's Eye) grape makes its traditional dry white. Newer estates such as Chateau Sungod and Great Wall Sanggan produce Bordeaux-style reds, and the Sino-French demonstration farm of 2001, now Domaine Franco-Chinois, brought Marselan to China.","terroir":"Dry, sunny and windy valley at about 500 m; cold winters require vine burial; sandy loam soils.","producers":["Great Wall (COFCO)","Chateau Sungod","Great Wall Sanggan Estate","Domaine Franco-Chinois"],"nerd_note":"Great Wall's dry white made from Longyan in the late 1970s, the result of a state research project, is usually cited as China's first modern dry white wine.","rslug":"hebei"},"cn-shangri-la-deqin":{"name":"Shangri-La (Deqin)","cat":"Producing area","subcat":"Producing area (no GI)","region":"Yunnan","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Cabernet Franc, Merlot, Syrah","summary":"High valleys of the upper Mekong and Jinsha rivers in Deqin County, under the sacred Meili Snow Mountains, where small vineyards at 2,200 to 2,600 m produce China's most expensive wine, LVMH's Ao Yun, a Cabernet-led blend first released (2013 vintage) in 2016. French missionaries first planted vines at Cizhong church in the nineteenth century.","terroir":"Dry valleys in the Himalayan rain shadow, strong sun, cold nights; river gravel and schist. Villages ripen weeks apart because of altitude and aspect.","producers":["Ao Yun","Shangri-La Winery"],"rslug":"yunnan"},"cn-tianshan-north-foothills":{"name":"Tianshan North Foothills","cat":"Producing area","subcat":"Producing area (no EU-protected GI)","region":"Xinjiang","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Syrah, Chardonnay","summary":"Vineyards along the northern foothills of the Tianshan around Changji, Manas and Shihezi, including CITIC Guoan's Suntime and Xinjiang Changyu's Baron Balboa estate. Large state-farm plantings from the 1970s supply both big brands and newer estates.","terroir":"Continental desert with hot summers and severe winters; alluvial soils; snowmelt irrigation.","producers":["Suntime","Chateau Changyu Baron Balboa","Chateau Ningmu"],"rslug":"xinjiang"},"cn-turpan":{"name":"Turpan","cat":"Producing area","subcat":"Producing area (GI proposed 2015)","region":"Xinjiang","styles":["red","white","sweet"],"grapes":"Thompson Seedless, Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Chardonnay","summary":"The Turpan depression, partly below sea level and one of the hottest places in Asia, has grown grapes for over 2,000 years; its raisins and seedless table grapes are famous throughout China. The Tang dynasty learned winemaking here after conquering Gaochang in 641. A small modern wine industry includes Loulan and Puchang; Turpan was proposed for national GI protection in 2015.","terroir":"Extremely hot, arid depression (summer highs above 45 degrees C), irrigated by karez underground canals; sandy soils.","producers":["Loulan","Puchang Vineyard"],"rslug":"xinjiang"},"cn-yanqi-basin":{"name":"Yanqi Basin","cat":"Producing area","subcat":"Producing area (no EU-protected GI)","region":"Xinjiang","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Marselan, Merlot, Riesling, Chardonnay","summary":"A basin on the southern side of the Tianshan near Bosten Lake and Korla, at about 1,000 to 1,200 m, which since the 2010s has become Xinjiang's leading quality area. Tiansai Vineyards and Chateau Zhongfei make structured, sunny reds. Hoxud County, in the basin, was proposed as a national GI in 2015.","terroir":"Desert continental climate, gravel and sandy soils, Tianshan snowmelt irrigation; vines buried in winter.","producers":["Tiansai","Chateau Zhongfei","Xiangdu"],"rslug":"xinjiang"},"cn-yantai":{"name":"Yantai","cat":"GI","subcat":"National GI (EU-protected)","region":"Shandong","created":"EU protection 2021","styles":["red","white","sweet"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Cabernet Gernischt, Merlot, Marselan, Chardonnay, Petit Manseng","summary":"The GI for wine from Yantai on the Jiaodong Peninsula, the birthplace of modern Chinese wine. Zhang Bishi founded Changyu here in 1892 and dug its Grand Cellar beside the sea. Today Yantai (with the Penglai district) has the largest concentration of wineries in China, from Changyu's headquarters and its Chateau Changyu-Castel to Domaines Barons de Rothschild's Long Dai in the Qiushan valley. Its maritime climate spares vines from burial but brings monsoon rain at harvest, so wines are typically lighter and fresher than Ningxia's.","terroir":"Hilly, granite- and sand-based soils near the Bohai and Yellow Sea; mild winters, humid summers.","producers":["Changyu","Chateau Changyu-Castel","Long Dai (Domaines Barons de Rothschild)","Treaty Port Vineyards","Chateau Junding"],"nerd_note":"Changyu's Cabernet Gernischt, a grape it imported in the 1890s, turned out on DNA testing to be Carmenère, which also went unrecognised in Chile until 1994.","rslug":"shandong"}}};
